<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cacaxtla</h1><p class="st-meta">650 CE – 950 CE (approx.) · capital Cacaxtla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A hilltop palace-city in Tlaxcala, seat of the Olmeca-Xicalanca, whose brilliant murals blend Maya and central Mexican styles.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Teotihuacan's decline, Cacaxtla rose around 650 on a fortified hill in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala</a>, alongside the neighbouring ceremonial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xochitecatl">Xochitécatl</a>. Later chronicles attribute it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmeca-xicalanca">Olmeca-Xicalanca</a>, a people with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> connections who controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puebla-tlaxcala-valley">Puebla-Tlaxcala valley</a> in this period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cacaxtla's Great Platform held palaces, plazas and shrines protected by ditches and walls. Its murals, painted between about 650 and 900, show a battle between jaguar and bird warriors and richly dressed figures with symbols of trade, in a style strongly influenced by the Maya. They suggest a cosmopolitan elite tied to long-distance trade between the highlands and the Gulf.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cacaxtla was abandoned around 950, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmeca-xicalanca">Olmeca-Xicalanca</a> were displaced by incoming groups, later including the founders of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala</a>. Its murals were discovered in 1975 and are among the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cacaxtla.jpg" alt="Cacaxtla" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Hajor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexico.Tlax.Cacaxtla.04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 CE – 950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cacaxtla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cacaxtla-Xochitecatl, Olmeca-Xicalanca state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=cacaxtla" aria-label="Cacaxtla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2022 1465 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cacaxtla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2342 1721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2342 1721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=cacaxtla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

